  "statement": "Expand $(1-2x)^{-2}$ in ascending powers of $x$ up to the term in $x^{3}$, and show that the coefficient of $x^{n}$ is $(n+1)2^{n}$.",
  "solution": "$(1+u)^{-2}=1-2u+3u^{2}-4u^{3}+\\cdots$, so with $u=-2x$: $(1-2x)^{-2}=1+4x+12x^{2}+32x^{3}+\\cdots$. In general $(1+u)^{-2}=\\sum_{n\\ge0}(-1)^{n}(n+1)u^{n}$, hence the coefficient of $x^{n}$ is $(-1)^{n}(n+1)(-2)^{n}=(n+1)2^{n}$. Checks: $n=1\\to4,\\; n=2\\to12,\\; n=3\\to32$.",
  "hints": [
    "Write the binomial series for $(1+u)^{-2}$.",
    "Substitute $u=-2x$ and read off the first coefficients.",
    "Generalise the coefficient pattern."
  ],
